       from categories where slug = 'help';
     values ('aae29f01-71de-47b6-9771-29c331e15c11', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', '3916339e-7bd6-43db-a5eb-5f518f9fc522', 'I sharpen to 8000 grit and the edge is gone after chopping a couple of mortises in oak. What am I doing wrong?', true, now() - interval '691200 seconds');
     values ('d2cb0ddc-878e-4a44-b82b-6a7e6e589a59', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', 'fc593465-0ef8-423f-be4c-c368c0817818', 'What angle? If you''re under 25 degrees for chopping, the edge will fold.', null, now() - interval '630631 seconds');
     values ('b588b413-0c14-4f9f-a209-d67a4c9360b5', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', '3916339e-7bd6-43db-a5eb-5f518f9fc522', 'About 20, I think. I use a honing guide set to what the video said.', 'd2cb0ddc-878e-4a44-b82b-6a7e6e589a59', now() - interval '542062 seconds');
     values ('6d90181d-49fa-47a3-9490-090fa7968ed9', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', 'e36648ad-ae34-420b-8177-6eff2bb3ad5b', 'For mortising in oak, hone a 30 to 35 degree micro bevel on a 25 primary. Paring chisels can be lower. Chopping needs more steel behind the edge.', null, now() - interval '438557 seconds');
     values ('a0064dc5-2481-443a-a796-e76c42095df5', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', '0f42ea3b-28d9-46ec-8aaa-7d8f615308b3', 'Also check you''re not rocking the chisel to lever out waste. That''s what snaps edges.', null, now() - interval '323979 seconds');
     values ('783932c7-8e50-458c-a246-ba10e37a9108', '4f148c72-c3cf-46d2-bb06-947a1f8f8dba', '3916339e-7bd6-43db-a5eb-5f518f9fc522', 'Went to a 32 micro bevel and it''s holding up so much better. Thanks everyone.', '6d90181d-49fa-47a3-9490-090fa7968ed9', now() - interval '200393 seconds');
